13:10:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your experience can be real without being representative of an entire nationality. A few reasons it can feel universal: - **Small and selective sample:** The Italians someone meets may cluster by age, region, workplace, or social setting. Body size varies considerably across Italy and across generations. - **Television isn’t a population sample:** Casting, camera angles, costumes, and the kinds of programs exported internationally can distort impressions. - **“Heavy” is subjective:** A person may look broad, muscular, stocky, or simply dressed in a way that reads as larger, without necessarily having high body fat. - **Confirmation bias:** Once you notice a pattern, examples that fit it stand out more than examples that do not. There is a statistical basis for saying excess weight is common among Italian adult men: national surveys have often found that around half of men fall into the combined “overweight or obesity” BMI categories, with rates higher in older groups. But that category includes many people who are only modestly above the standard BMI cutoff, and it certainly does not describe all Italian men—or establish that being heavily built is an inherent Italian trait. So the fair version is: *your cousin may be seeing a common pattern in a particular setting, and overweight is common among adult Italian men, but it’s not accurate to treat every Italian person or Italian men generally as uniformly heavy.*
